        "Attributes": [
          {
            "Id": 758473,
            "attributeType": "LongString",
            "attributeName": "AIDescription",
            "attributeRelationType": "None",
            "Value": "The Sega Master System, initially released in Japan as the Sega Mark III, is an 8-bit home video game console that marked Sega's resurgence in the console market. Launched in 1986, it featured a Zilog Z80A processor and a custom graphics chip enabling a resolution of 256x192 pixels and a palette of 64 colors. Games were distributed via ROM cartridges (up to 4MB) and smaller Sega Cards (32KB). While initially struggling against Nintendo's NES, the Master System found significant success in Europe and Brazil, boasting a larger game library than its North American counterpart, including ports of Genesis titles and exclusive titles like *Streets of Rage* and *The Lucky Dime Caper*. In Brazil, Tectoy localized many games, creating unique versions tied to popular local franchises. A successor to the SG-1000, the Master System laid the groundwork for Sega's future console dominance."
          },
